#: src/foundations/ownership.md:3
msgid ""
"You already know the core idea of this chapter. In C++ you write a class "
"whose constructor acquires a resource and whose destructor releases it, and "
"you rely on scope to run that destructor at the right time. That is RAII, "
"and it is the best idea C++ has."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:8
msgid ""
"Rust takes RAII and makes it the **only** way resources are managed, then "
"adds one thing C++ never had: the compiler tracks, for every value, which "
"variable currently owns it. That one addition removes use-after-move, double"
" free and most of the rule of five."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:13 src/foundations/borrowing.md:10
msgid "This chapter covers:"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:15
msgid "the three ownership rules, and how they map onto RAII,"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:16
msgid ""
"what a move actually does in each language (they are surprisingly "
"different),"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:17
msgid "`Copy` and `Clone`, the replacements for copy constructors,"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:18
msgid "`Drop`, the replacement for destructors, and drop order,"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:19
msgid "wrapping a GPU resource so it cannot leak or be freed twice."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:21
msgid "The rules"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:23
msgid "Every value has exactly one **owner**, a variable or a field."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:24
msgid ""
"When the owner goes out of scope, the value is **dropped** (its destructor "
"runs)."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:25
msgid ""
"Ownership can be **moved** to another variable, and then the old one can no "
"longer be used."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:28
msgid ""
"Rules 1 and 2 are RAII exactly as you know it. Rule 3 is where Rust and C++ "
"part ways."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:31
msgid "What a move does"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:33
msgid ""
"Here is the same function in both languages. Step through each tab, and pay "
"attention to what happens to `a`."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:38
msgid "Two differences matter:"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:42
msgid "Who performs the move"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:42
msgid "The move constructor, user code that can do anything"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:42
msgid "The compiler: a bitwise copy of the value's bytes"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:43
msgid "State of the source"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:43
msgid "A live object in a \"valid but unspecified\" state"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:43
msgid "Statically inaccessible; using it is a compile error"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:44
msgid "Destructor of the source"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:44
msgid "Still runs, so the move constructor must neutralize it"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:44
msgid "Does not run"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:45
msgid "Can a move throw / fail?"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:45
msgid "Yes, unless marked `noexcept`"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:45
msgid "No, it is a `memcpy`"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:47
msgid ""
"Because a Rust move is always a shallow byte copy, every type is movable and"
" no type can customize how it moves. You never write a move constructor, and"
" `std::vector`'s old dilemma (copy elements on reallocation unless the move "
"constructor is `noexcept`) does not exist: `Vec` always moves elements with "
"`memcpy`."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:56
msgid ""
"\"Bitwise copy\" describes the semantics, not necessarily the machine code. "
"The optimizer removes most moves entirely, just as C++ elides copies. If a "
"move does show up in a profile, it is a `memcpy` of `size_of::<T>()` bytes. "
"A `Vec<T>` is 24 bytes on a 64-bit target no matter how many elements it "
"holds, so moving one is cheap. A `[f32; 4096]` is 16 KiB, so move it into a "
"`Box` first if you pass it around by value."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:65
msgid ""
"Try it yourself. This program does not compile. Run it to see the error, "
"then fix it by removing the bad line, or by using `let b = a.clone();` to "
"make an explicit deep copy."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:73
msgid "\"a has {} elements\""
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:74
msgid "\"b has {} elements\""
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:78
msgid "Moves into and out of functions"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:80
msgid ""
"Passing an argument by value moves it, exactly like passing a "
"`std::unique_ptr<T>` by value. There is no `std::move` to write, because the"
" move is the default, and there is no null left behind."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:86
msgid ""
"Returning a value moves it out to the caller, which is how a function hands "
"ownership back:"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:93
msgid "// moved out to the caller; nothing is freed here\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:100
msgid "// ownership comes back\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:105
msgid "// move in, move out\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:106
msgid "\"{buf:?}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:110
msgid ""
"Taking and giving back ownership works, but it is clumsy when a function "
"only needs to look at or modify a value. That is what borrowing is for, and "
"it is the subject of the [next chapter](borrowing.md). In practice most "
"functions take references, and you move only when ownership really changes "
"hands: pushing into a container, sending to another thread, storing in a "
"struct."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:119
msgid ""
"In C++, `void f(std::string s)` followed by `f(name)` silently copies the "
"string, and you add `std::move` to avoid it. In Rust, `f(name)` moves, and "
"you write `f(name.clone())` when you really want a copy. Expensive "
"operations are always visible in the source."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:126
msgid "Copy types"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:128
msgid ""
"Moving an `i32` and then using the original would be silly: there is nothing"
" to \"own\". Types whose bytes can be duplicated safely implement the `Copy`"
" trait, and assignment copies them instead of moving them. The source stays "
"usable."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:146
msgid "// a copy, not a move\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:147 src/foundations/borrowing.md:143
#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:172 src/foundations/borrowing.md:173
#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:174
msgid "\"{}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:147
msgid "// `up` is still usable\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:148
msgid "\"{also_up:?}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:152
msgid ""
"Integers, floats, `bool`, `char`, shared references, and arrays and tuples "
"of `Copy` types are all `Copy`. A type can only be `Copy` if all its fields "
"are, and if it has no `Drop` implementation. `String`, `Vec` and `Box` are "
"never `Copy`, because duplicating their bytes would create two owners of one"
" heap allocation."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:158
msgid ""
"The rule of thumb for engine code: small plain-data types such as vectors, "
"matrices, colors, handles and IDs should derive `Copy`. Anything that owns a"
" resource should not."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/ownership.md:162
msgid ""
"`Clone` is the explicit deep copy, the equivalent of a copy constructor that"
" you must call by name. `#[derive(Clone)]` generates a member-wise clone, "
"like a defaulted copy constructor."
msgstr ""

