#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:3
msgid ""
"Moving ownership every time a function needs to look at a value would be "
"unworkable. Most of the time you want what C++ gives you with `const T&` and"
" `T&`: access without ownership. Rust calls this **borrowing**, and its "
"references look like C++ references with one big addition: the compiler "
"checks that a reference can never outlive what it points to, and that nobody"
" modifies data while someone else is reading it."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:12
msgid "the two kinds of reference and the one rule that governs them,"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:13
msgid "why that rule rules out iterator and reference invalidation,"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:14
msgid "how long a borrow lasts,"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:15
msgid "slices, the safe `std::span`,"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:16
msgid "what the rule buys the optimizer,"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:17
msgid ""
"the borrow-checker fights you will have in engine code, and their fixes."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:19
msgid "Two kinds of reference"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:32
msgid "// nothing at the call site says it mutates\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:51
msgid "// mutation is visible here\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:52
msgid "\"{a} {samples:?}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:59
msgid ""
"`&T` is a **shared reference**: read-only access, like `const T&`. You can "
"have as many as you like at the same time."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:61
msgid ""
"`&mut T` is a **mutable reference**: read-write access, like `T&`. While it "
"exists, it is the _only_ way to reach the value."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:64
msgid ""
"Both are plain pointers at run time, never null, and always point to a "
"valid, initialized value. Unlike C++ references they are first-class values:"
" you can reassign a `let` binding holding a reference, store references in "
"structs, and put them in containers."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:69
msgid ""
"You also borrow explicitly at the call site: `average(&samples)`, "
"`normalize(&mut samples)`. Reading a call tells you whether it can modify "
"its argument, which C++ hides behind the function signature."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:73
msgid "The rule: shared XOR mutable"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:75
msgid ""
"At any point in the program, a value can have **either** any number of `&T` "
"**or** exactly one `&mut T`, never both. Step through it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:80
msgid ""
"While a shared borrow is in use, the owner is also frozen against writes: "
"`scores` itself cannot be modified until `a` and `b` are done. While a "
"mutable borrow is in use, the owner cannot even be _read_ except through it."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:84
msgid ""
"This is a reader-writer lock, enforced at compile time, with zero runtime "
"cost."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:86
msgid "The bug this rule removes"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:88
msgid ""
"Every C++ engineer has hit this, usually with `std::vector<Entity>` and a "
"cached `Entity*` or iterator. Compare the two tabs:"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:93
msgid ""
"The C++ program is undefined behavior that usually _appears_ to work. The "
"Rust program does not compile, and the error points at all three lines "
"involved: where the borrow starts, where the conflicting mutation is, and "
"where the borrow is used later."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:98
msgid ""
"The same rule covers modifying a container while iterating over it, holding "
"a `string_view` into a `std::string` that gets reassigned, and a `std::span`"
" over a vector that grows. These are all the same mistake: a reader holds a "
"pointer into memory that a writer is about to move or free."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:103
msgid "How long does a borrow last?"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:105
msgid ""
"A borrow is alive from where it is created to its **last use**, not to the "
"end of the enclosing block. The compiler computes this (\"non-lexical "
"lifetimes\"), and most of the time you never think about it. When you do get"
" an error, picture the live range of each reference. Hover the bars below to"
" see which lines each borrow covers, and switch between the rejected program"
" and two fixes:"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:114
