#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:178
msgid ""
"Indexing a slice is bounds-checked. An out-of-range index panics instead of "
"reading past the end. In hot loops, iterating (`for x in xs`) avoids the "
"checks entirely, and the [iterators chapter](closures-and-iterators.md) "
"shows how to confirm that in the assembly."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:183
msgid "What the rule buys the optimizer"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:185
msgid ""
"Because a `&mut T` is guaranteed to be the only path to its target, the "
"compiler can assume it does not alias anything else. In C++ terms, every "
"`&mut` is `__restrict`, and the compiler checks it for you."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:197
msgid ""
"// must reload b: a and b\n"
"              // might be the same int\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:210
msgid ""
"// b cannot alias a: the\n"
"              // compiler folds this to\n"
"              // *a += 2 * *b\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:220
msgid ""
"Open the Rust version in the Playground (link under the example), choose "
"**Show Assembly** in release mode, and compare with the C++ version in "
"Compiler Explorer. The Rust version loads `b` once. In SIMD-heavy code such "
"as skinning, particle updates or audio mixing, this is the difference "
"between vectorizing and not, without sprinkling `__restrict` around and "
"hoping it is true."
msgstr ""

