#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:293
msgid ""
"The fixes, in order of preference: access the fields directly inside the "
"`impl`; write one method that returns both borrows at once (`fn "
"parts_mut(&mut self) -> (&mut A, &B)`); or split the struct so the parts "
"that change together live together. The last one is the road to an ECS, "
"which has [its own chapter](../engine/ecs.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:299
msgid "Long-lived pointers between objects"
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:301
msgid ""
"Storing `&Enemy` inside `Missile` to remember its target works for a few "
"lines of code and then fails, because the borrow would freeze the whole "
"enemy list for as long as the missile exists. References are for _temporary_"
" access. For long-lived relationships between objects, use [handles into an "
"arena](../engine/handles-and-arenas.md), or shared ownership with "
"[`Rc`/`Arc`](smart-pointers.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/foundations/borrowing.md:311
msgid ""
"In C++ it is normal to keep a raw pointer to \"the thing I'm working with\" "
"as a member. In Rust, a struct that holds a reference needs a lifetime "
"parameter and borrows its target for its whole life. That is occasionally "
"right (parsers, views, iterators), but for game objects it almost never is. "
"If you find yourself adding `<'a>` to a struct to make the borrow checker "
"happy, step back and ask who should own the data."
msgstr ""

